## The Outbox Synchronization Pattern

To guarantee that user mutations are never lost when operating in poor connectivity environments (warehouses, agricultural fields, elevators), applications must decouple user actions from network availability using the **Outbox Pattern**:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph Client["1. Mobile Client Execution"]
        UI["User Action\n(e.g., Submit Order)"]
        LocalDB["Local Entity Store\n(Room / CoreData / WatermelonDB)"]
        OutboxQueue["Durable Outbox Queue\n(Idempotency Key + Payload + Status)"]

        UI -->|"1. Optimistic write"| LocalDB
        UI -->|"2. Enqueue mutation"| OutboxQueue
        LocalDB -->|"3. Instant render"| UI
    end

    subgraph SyncWorker["2. Background Synchronization Engine"]
        Scheduler["Background Task Scheduler\n(WorkManager / BGAppRefresh / NetInfo)"]
        Batcher["Outbox Drainer\n(Reads 'PENDING' records ordered by timestamp)"]
        Scheduler -->|"Radio connected & unmetered"| Batcher
        OutboxQueue -.->|"Pulls pending items"| Batcher
    end

    subgraph Backend["3. Enterprise Cloud Core"]
        Gateway["Enterprise API Gateway\n(Validates Idempotency-Key header)"]
        Microservice["Backend Service & Database"]

        Batcher -->|"HTTP POST /mutations\nX-Idempotency-Key: UUID"| Gateway
        Gateway -->|"Idempotent transaction"| Microservice
        Microservice -->>|"200 OK (Authoritative state)"| Batcher
        Batcher -->|"Mark 'SYNCED' / Prune"| OutboxQueue
    end

