## Conflict Resolution Protocols

When data is modified on a mobile device while offline while simultaneously changing on the server, the synchronization engine must resolve discrepancies without corrupting business state:

<div class="portal-table-wrap">
  <table class="portal-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Resolution Strategy</th>
        <th>Applicable Domain</th>
        <th>Resolution Mechanism</th>
        <th>Mitigation for Lost Updates</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>1. Server-Authoritative (Strict)</strong></td>
        <td>Financial balances, inventory counts, order statuses.</td>
        <td>Server rejects conflicting client mutation (HTTP 409 Conflict). Client rolls back local optimistic state and alerts user.</td>
        <td>Full audit log of rejected mutation saved locally for user review.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>2. Last-Write-Wins (LWW)</strong></td>
        <td>User profile info, notification preferences, draft notes.</td>
        <td>Compares monotonic server timestamps (<code>updated_at</code>). The mutation with the latest timestamp overwrites prior state.</td>
        <td>Requires synchronized NTP server timestamps; never rely on mobile device local clock.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>3. Field-Level Merge</strong></td>
        <td>Complex forms, inspection reports, multi-section surveys.</td>
        <td>Patches only the dirty fields modified locally. Unmodified fields retain server-authoritative values.</td>
        <td>Reduces conflict probability from whole-record collisions down to individual field overlaps.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

